{"name":"Silver Screen","hex":"#a6aeaa","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver screen","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An early type of cinema screen which had a coating of silver or another shiny metal.","Any cinema screen onto which films are projected."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_screen"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver screen","blocks":[{"text":"A silver screen, also known as a silver lenticular screen, is a type of projection screen that was popular in the early years of the motion picture industry and passed into popular usage as a metonym for the cinema industry. The term silver screen comes from the actual silver content embedded in the material that made up the screen's highly reflective surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/3d-silberleinwand.jpg/330px-3d-silberleinwand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_screen"},{"title":"Silverscreen","blocks":[{"text":"Silverscreen was a British chain store brand founded in 2003 in the United Kingdom and focused on films. It was founded by Ernesto Schmitt and Sebastian James."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silverscreen"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"},{"title":"Silver (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver or metallic gray is a color tone resembling gray that is a representation of the color of polished silver."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png/330px-Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Victory_Theatre%2C_Sydney%2C_1930%27s_-_Sam_Hood_%283464323081%29.jpg/500px-Victory_Theatre%2C_Sydney%2C_1930%27s_-_Sam_Hood_%283464323081%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Victory_Theatre%2C_Sydney%2C_1930%27s_-_Sam_Hood_%283464323081%29.jpg/500px-Victory_Theatre%2C_Sydney%2C_1930%27s_-_Sam_Hood_%283464323081%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A silver screen as seen in Victory Theatre (Sydney) in the 1930s.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVictory_Theatre%2C_Sydney%2C_1930's_-_Sam_Hood_(3464323081).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg/500px-1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg/500px-1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silver bullion bar, 1000 ounces","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ag_atomic_wire.jpg/500px-Ag_atomic_wire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=th